Below is a Java version of the dice-sum simulator that:
- Prints the theoretical distribution for sums 2..12
- Simulates rolling two dice 36, 500, and 10,000 times
- Uses arrays for organization (ways[], theoretical probabilities[], counts[])
- Uses DecimalFormat for rounding
- Uses if-statements to adjust tabulation based on number lengths

```python
// Java program: Sum of 2 Dice Simulator
// - Theoretical probabilities for sums 2..12
// - Simulates 36, 500, and 10,000 rolls
// - Uses DecimalFormat for rounding
// - Adjusts tabulation with if-statements based on number lengths

import java.text.DecimalFormat;
import java.util.Random;

public class DiceSumSimulator {

    // Build theoretical probability distribution for sums 2..12
    // Uses arrays to store number of ways and probabilities.
    static double[] theoreticalDistribution() {
        double[] probs = new double[13]; // index by sum (2..12)
        int[] ways = new int[13];        // index by sum (2..12)

        // ways[s] = number of ways to roll sum s with two dice
        ways[2] = 1;  ways[3] = 2;  ways[4] = 3;  ways[5] = 4;  ways[6] = 5;
        ways[7] = 6;  ways[8] = 5;  ways[9] = 4;  ways[10] = 3; ways[11] = 2; ways[12] = 1;

        int totalOutcomes = 36;
        for (int s = 2; s <= 12; s++) {
            probs[s] = ways[s] / (double) totalOutcomes;
        }
        return probs;
    }

    // Simulate rolling two fair six-sided dice numRolls times.
    // Returns counts of each sum (2..12) in an array indexed by sum.
    static int[] simulateTwoDice(int numRolls, Random rng) {
        int[] counts = new int[13]; // index by sum (2..12)
        for (int i = 0; i < numRolls; i++) {
            int d1 = 1 + rng.nextInt(6);
            int d2 = 1 + rng.nextInt(6);
            counts[d1 + d2]++;
        }
        return counts;
    }

    static void printTheoreticalTable(double[] theory, DecimalFormat df) {
        System.out.println("Theoretical probability distribution for X = sum of two dice");
        System.out.println("X\tP(X)");
        for (int s = 2; s <= 12; s++) {
            // Adjust tabs based on number of digits in X (single vs double digit)
            String tabsAfterX = (s < 10) ? "\t\t" : "\t";
            System.out.println(s + tabsAfterX + df.format(theory[s]));
        }
        System.out.println();
    }

    static void printSimulationTable(int[] counts, double[] theory, int numRolls, DecimalFormat df) {
        System.out.println("Empirical distribution from simulation (num rolls = " + numRolls + ")");
        System.out.println("X\tCount\t\tEm Prob\t\tP(X)");

        for (int s = 2; s <= 12; s++) {
            int count = counts[s];
            double emProb = count / (double) numRolls;

            // Tabs after X: if X is single digit, use extra tab for alignment
            String tabsAfterX = (s < 10) ? "\t\t" : "\t";

            // Tabs after Count: adjust based on the length of count
            String tabsAfterCount;
            if (count < 10) {
                tabsAfterCount = "\t\t\t";     // very small numbers get more tabs
            } else if (count < 100) {
                tabsAfterCount = "\t\t";       // two-digit
            } else if (count < 1000) {
                tabsAfterCount = "\t\t";       // three-digit
            } else {
                tabsAfterCount = "\t";         // four or more digits
            }

            System.out.println(
                s + tabsAfterX +
                count + tabsAfterCount +
                df.format(emProb) + "\t\t" +
                df.format(theory[s])
            );
        }
        System.out.println();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        DecimalFormat df = new DecimalFormat("#.####");

        // Theoretical answers
        System.out.println("Total possible outcomes with two dice: 36");
        System.out.println("Theoretical probabilities:");
        System.out.println("P(2)=1/36, P(3)=2/36, P(4)=3/36, P(5)=4/36, P(6)=5/36, P(7)=6/36,");
        System.out.println("P(8)=5/36, P(9)=4/36, P(10)=3/36, P(11)=2/36, P(12)=1/36");
        System.out.println("Expected percent with sum 7: 16.6667%");
        System.out.println();

        // Build theoretical distribution using arrays
        double[] theory = theoreticalDistribution();
        printTheoreticalTable(theory, df);

        // Simulations
        Random rng = new Random(); // For reproducible runs, use new Random(seed)
        int[] trials = {36, 500, 10_000};
        for (int n : trials) {
            int[] counts = simulateTwoDice(n, rng);
            printSimulationTable(counts, theory, n, df);
        }

        // Notes:
        // - Count: how many rolls resulted in each sum X
        // - Em Prob: empirical probability (count / numberRolled)
        // - P(X): theoretical probability
    }
}
```

Notes on formatting:
- DecimalFormat is used to round probabilities to 4 decimal places (pattern "#.####" as in your hint).
- If-statements adjust tabs based on the number of digits in X and Count to keep columns aligned reasonably across small and large simulations.

Below is a self-contained C++ program that:
- Generates a vector of random integers.
- Sorts it using Selection Sort and measures the running time.
- Builds two BSTs:
  - Unbalanced BST by inserting the sorted values in order (worst-case shape).
  - Balanced BST built from the sorted array via divide-and-conquer (optimal shape).
- Measures and reports the build times and heights.

Code is provided in a python-tagged code block as requested, but it is C++ code.

```python
// C++17
// Build a BST "using Selection Sort": we selection-sort the data first,
// then build both an unbalanced BST (inserting sorted data) and a balanced BST (from sorted array).
// We measure running times for sorting and both build approaches.

#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <random>
#include <chrono>
#include <algorithm>
#include <iomanip>
#include <cstdlib>

struct Node {
    int key;
    Node* left;
    Node* right;
    explicit Node(int k) : key(k), left(nullptr), right(nullptr) {}
};

Node* insertBST(Node* root, int key) {
    if (!root) return new Node(key);
    if (key < root->key) {
        root->left = insertBST(root->left, key);
    } else {
        // Put duplicates to the right
        root->right = insertBST(root->right, key);
    }
    return root;
}

Node* buildBSTFromSortedInsert(const std::vector<int>& a) {
    Node* root = nullptr;
    for (int x : a) root = insertBST(root, x);
    return root;
}

Node* buildBalancedBSTFromSorted(const std::vector<int>& a, int l, int r) {
    if (l > r) return nullptr;
    int m = l + (r - l) / 2;
    Node* root = new Node(a[m]);
    root->left = buildBalancedBSTFromSorted(a, l, m - 1);
    root->right = buildBalancedBSTFromSorted(a, m + 1, r);
    return root;
}

int height(Node* root) {
    if (!root) return 0;
    int hl = height(root->left);
    int hr = height(root->right);
    return 1 + (hl > hr ? hl : hr);
}

void destroy(Node* root) {
    if (!root) return;
    destroy(root->left);
    destroy(root->right);
    delete root;
}

void inorder(Node* root, std::vector<int>& out) {
    if (!root) return;
    inorder(root->left, out);
    out.push_back(root->key);
    inorder(root->right, out);
}

void selection_sort(std::vector<int>& a) {
    const size_t n = a.size();
    for (size_t i = 0; i + 1 < n; ++i) {
        size_t min_idx = i;
        for (size_t j = i + 1; j < n; ++j) {
            if (a[j] < a[min_idx]) min_idx = j;
        }
        if (min_idx != i) std::swap(a[i], a[min_idx]);
    }
}

int main(int argc, char** argv) {
    // Config
    size_t N = 5000; // default size; selection sort is O(N^2), so keep modest
    if (argc > 1) {
        long long t = std::atoll(argv[1]);
        if (t > 0) N = static_cast<size_t>(t);
    }

    // Generate random data
    std::mt19937 rng(std::random_device{}());
    std::uniform_int_distribution<int> dist(0, 1'000'000);

    std::vector<int> data;
    data.reserve(N);
    for (size_t i = 0; i < N; ++i) data.push_back(dist(rng));

    // Copy for sorting
    std::vector<int> sorted = data;

    // Time Selection Sort
    auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    selection_sort(sorted);
    auto t1 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();

    auto sort_us = std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::microseconds>(t1 - t0).count();

    // Build unbalanced BST by inserting sorted values (worst-case shape)
    auto b0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    Node* bst_unbalanced = buildBSTFromSortedInsert(sorted);
    auto b1 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    auto build_unbalanced_us = std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::microseconds>(b1 - b0).count();
    int h_unbalanced = height(bst_unbalanced);

    // Build balanced BST from sorted array (optimal shape)
    auto bb0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    Node* bst_balanced = buildBalancedBSTFromSorted(sorted, 0, static_cast<int>(sorted.size()) - 1);
    auto bb1 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    auto build_balanced_us = std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::microseconds>(bb1 - bb0).count();
    int h_balanced = height(bst_balanced);

    // Verify inorder of either BST is sorted (use balanced to keep stack depth lower)
    std::vector<int> inorder_out;
    inorder_out.reserve(N);
    inorder(bst_balanced, inorder_out);
    bool is_sorted_ok = std::is_sorted(inorder_out.begin(), inorder_out.end());

    // Output summary
    std::cout << "N = " << N << "\n";
    std::cout << "Selection sort time: " << sort_us << " microseconds\n";
    std::cout << "Build unbalanced BST (sorted insert) time: " << build_unbalanced_us << " microseconds, height = " << h_unbalanced << "\n";
    std::cout << "Build balanced BST (from sorted) time: " << build_balanced_us << " microseconds, height = " << h_balanced << "\n";
    std::cout << "Inorder check sorted: " << (is_sorted_ok ? "OK" : "FAIL") << "\n";

    // Show first few inorder values to confirm (optional)
    size_t show = std::min<size_t>(10, inorder_out.size());
    std::cout << "First " << show << " inorder values: ";
    for (size_t i = 0; i < show; ++i) std::cout << inorder_out[i] << (i + 1 < show ? ' ' : '\n');

    // Cleanup
    destroy(bst_unbalanced);
    destroy(bst_balanced);

    return 0;
}
```

How it works:
- Selection Sort: Pure O(n^2) algorithm; we time it with std::chrono::steady_clock.
- Unbalanced BST: Inserting already-sorted data into a normal BST yields a degenerate tree (height ≈ n). This demonstrates how insertion order impacts BST shape.
- Balanced BST: Since we have a sorted array, we can build an optimal-height BST in O(n) by always choosing the middle element as root recursively.
- Timings: Reported in microseconds. Adjust N via command line if you want different sizes.

Build and run:
- Compile: g++ -O2 -std=c++17 bst_selection_sort.cpp -o bst_selection_sort
- Run: ./bst_selection_sort 5000
- Note: For large N, selection sort will be slow by design.

Here are the main issues to watch out for in your current program and specific ways to address them:

- Risk of stack overflow on large N due to deep recursion
  - The unbalanced BST insertion from sorted data produces a degenerate chain; recursive insert, height, inorder, and destroy can reach recursion depth ≈ N and overflow the stack.
  - Fix: Replace recursive routines with iterative versions for insert, height, inorder, and destroy. Optionally also make the balanced build iterative (log N recursion is generally safe, but you can avoid recursion entirely).

- Timing resolution and repeatability
  - For small N, microsecond timing can be noisy, and results can show “0 microseconds.” Consider optional repeats or larger N for the sort timing.
  - Warn or guard against very large N because selection sort is O(N^2) and can take a long time.

- Index types and general robustness
  - Use size_t for indices to avoid signed overflow concerns when N grows.
  - Keep consistent time source (steady_clock) and avoid mixing clock types.

- Fairness and measurement clarity
  - Building the unbalanced BST by sorted insert is intended to show worst case but is O(N^2) and slow; that’s OK, but mention it and avoid huge N.
  - Dynamic allocation overhead dominates build timings; if you need cleaner timing, consider a node pool/arena (not included below for brevity).

