Below is a focused guide on integrating Firebase with your Next.js (App Router) setup for documentation management, with emphasis on understanding your existing codebase and Firebase setup before proceeding. It also includes drop-in code you can adapt.

Read this first: understand your current codebase
- Locate your Firebase initialization (usually at "@/lib/firebase"). Confirm:
  - It initializes the Web SDK (not Admin SDK) for client-side usage.
  - It exports db (Firestore) and storage (Firebase Storage).
  - Your .env.local has NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefixed Firebase config values.
- Confirm your Next.js routing: the App Router (app/ directory).
- Tailwind: Ensure it's configured; for best markdown rendering consider @tailwindcss/typography and use the prose class.
- Verify where you use client-side hooks (useState/useEffect). In files that use them, add the directive "use client" at the top. Note: It’s a directive string, not a hook.

Key Firebase integration concepts in this setup
- Client-side Firestore reads/writes:
  - We use the Firebase Web SDK from client components. This gives real-time updates using onSnapshot.
  - Protect via Firestore Security Rules (see examples below).
- Firebase Storage for images:
  - We upload images to docs/{slug}/images/* and get public download URLs to embed in markdown.
- Data model:
  - Collection: docs
  - Doc ID: slug (e.g., getting-started)
  - Fields: { title: string, content: string (markdown), tags: string[], createdAt: serverTimestamp, updatedAt: serverTimestamp }

