Below is a step-by-step guide and a complete, commented C# example you can run inside an Advance Steel detailed drawing to create custom Cartesian (ordinate) dimensions for every circular hole in a plate. The code ensures that all dimensions reference a single origin: the bottom-left corner of the plate outline in the selected view.

High-level, how this works in Advance Steel
- Why use the AutoCAD .NET API: Advance Steel drawings are DWG-based. The public Advance Steel .NET API does not expose methods to directly place ordinate dimensions on drawings. Therefore, the dimension entities are created using the AutoCAD .NET API (DatabaseServices.OrdinateDimension).
- Where the Advance Steel .NET API fits: Use it to establish context and to work inside Advance Steel drawings. The sample shows wrapping execution in Autodesk.AdvanceSteel.CADAccess.DocumentContext so you operate with the Advance Steel runtime loaded and its databases available. If you want to discover/manage drawing documents and views through Advance Steel types, look under Autodesk.AdvanceSteel.Drawing.*; this sample does not require those types because it works on the selected view block directly.

Step-by-step guide
1) Prepare references and environment
   - Reference assemblies: AcCoreMgd.dll, AcDbMgd.dll, AcMgd.dll (AutoCAD), and AstorMgd.dll (Advance Steel managed API).
   - Use Autodesk.AdvanceSteel.CADAccess.DocumentContext to ensure the Advance Steel runtime context is active when your command runs.

2) Pick the target view
   - Prompt the user to select the Advance Steel single-part view block in paper space.
   - Use Autodesk.AutoCAD.EditorInput.Editor.GetEntity to retrieve the BlockReference.

3) Access the view contents
   - Open the BlockReference.BlockTableRecord to access all entities inside the view block.
   - Traverse the view’s nested geometry (recursively) and collect:
     - Closed polylines for the plate outline.
     - Circles for hole centers.

4) Compute the common origin
   - Transform candidate entities to paper space with the cumulative BlockReference.BlockTransform.
   - Choose the largest closed polyline by absolute area as the plate outline.
   - Compute GeometricExtents of that outline and set origin = (extents.MinPoint.X, extents.MinPoint.Y, 0). This is the plate’s bottom-left in the view.

5) Detect holes and test containment
   - For each circle found in the view, transform it to paper space.
   - Filter to those whose centers lie inside the plate outline polygon (point-in-polygon test).

6) Clean up previously-added dims (idempotence)
   - Register a RegApp name (XData) and erase any existing ordinate dimensions this command created in earlier runs.

7) Create the ordinate dimensions
   - For each hole center in paper space:
     - Create an X-ordinate dimension with origin at the computed bottom-left and a leader pointing to the right side of the plate.
     - Create a Y-ordinate dimension with origin at the same bottom-left and a leader pointing above the top of the plate.
   - Use DatabaseServices.OrdinateDimension with the bool useXAxis constructor parameter.

8) Apply styles and standards
   - Assign a dimstyle (e.g., db.Dimstyle), target layer, text options, etc.

9) Commit and review
   - Commit the transaction so dimensions are placed.
   - Rerun the command after updates; it will delete and recreate its own dimensions.

10) Extending to more cases
   - For slotted holes or complex cutouts, also look for Ellipse or Polyline hole representation; compute centers accordingly.
   - If you prefer model-driven accuracy, obtain hole centers from the model (Advance Steel modeling API) and project them into the view; then place the dimensions as below.

