Why this is “most accurate at each point”
- Interior points (i = 3..N−2) use the 5-point centered O(dx^4) stencil.
- The two near-boundary points (i = 2 and N−1) use the 3-point centered O(dx^2) stencil.
- Endpoints (i = 1 and N) use 4-point one-sided O(dx^2) stencils.
This combination is standard when you must compute y'' at every grid point with the highest feasible accuracy per location.

With these corrections, your second derivative should agree closely with the analytical result; any small discrepancies are due to the unavoidable lower-order boundary stencils.

