Here are the key limitations when you do this with the free versions of Ultimate Member and Meta Box, along with alternatives and best practices. A compact plugin example is provided at the end to show how to integrate a “one Artiste per user” create/edit/delete flow inside a UM profile tab using only core WordPress APIs.

Limitations with Ultimate Member (free)
- No built-in post submission/editing UI: UM Free manages user registration/profile forms, not custom post types. You must hand-roll your CPT form and processing (or use another front-end form plugin).
- Profile tab builder is manual: You can add a custom tab via code (filters/actions), but there’s no GUI for adding dynamic CPT forms to profile tabs in the free version.
- No native post listing/management in profile: You have to render your own list/details or wire up a third-party shortcode inside the UM tab.
- Permissions need extra care: UM roles don’t automatically grant CPT capabilities. Ensure your CPT caps and user roles line up, or rely on author-ownership plus default caps.

Limitations with Meta Box (free)
- No MB Frontend Submission: The free core doesn’t provide front-end form generation for posts. You need to build your own form markup and saving logic (insert/update post, update_post_meta, handle uploads).
- No unified front-end validation/UI: You’ll implement server-side validation and minimalist front-end markup yourself.
- Advanced field UX: Some “enhanced” field experiences (e.g., advanced media pickers) and builders are in premium extensions; with free, you’ll likely stick to simple inputs and handle uploads via WP core.

Other practical constraints
- Media uploads must be implemented manually via wp_handle_upload and set_post_thumbnail; you don’t get the admin media modal on the front end by default.
- “One per user” has edge cases (trashed posts, revisions) you must enforce via explicit queries.
- Capability mapping: If you set custom capabilities in register_post_type, you must grant them to the UM roles. If not, rely on author ownership and default caps to allow editing only by the owner.

Alternative approaches
- Use Meta Box Frontend Submission (premium): Lets you define your fields and output a working front-end form with much less code. You can then embed the form via shortcode inside your UM tab.
- Use a front-end form plugin that creates posts:
  - Gravity Forms + Post Creation Add-On, WS Form, Formidable, or Ninja Forms. They generate the CPT and meta creation/update flow, including media uploads, validation, and permissions. Embed their shortcode in the UM tab.
  - Advanced Custom Fields (ACF) with acf_form for front-end CPT editing. Embed acf_form() output within your tab callback or via a shortcode.
- Consider a REST/AJAX approach: Build a small REST endpoint for create/update/delete and a JS-driven form inside the UM tab for a smoother UX, with nonce-protected requests and immediate validation feedback.

Best practices for this integration
- Keep it as a small plugin (not in theme) so your logic survives theme switches and is easier to maintain.
- Enforce ownership via post_author and query for existing posts to implement “one per user” cleanly.
- Use server-side validation and strict capability checks (current_user_can with edit_post and delete_post).
- Use nonces for all state-changing actions and restrict allowed MIME types and file sizes for uploads.
- Decide on publish workflow (draft/pending/publish) and stick to it; show clear UI messages for status.
- If you add custom capabilities to your CPT, set map_meta_cap to true, then explicitly assign those capabilities to the UM roles that should manage Artiste.

