Operational notes
- Health and readiness: expose /health; add a /ready that only returns OK once STORE != null in in-memory mode or Mongo connection is healthy.
- File watch: if you enable chokidar, debounce changes and ensure atomic file writes upstream to avoid partial reads.
- Security: gate reload with X-Admin-Token; add rate limiting on /lookup if this is internet-facing; validate field/value inputs against an allowlist.
- Testing: golden files for XML; contract tests for response shape; load tests for P95 targets; chaos test reload during traffic.
- Rollout: deploy the new service behind a feature flag; mirror a % of traffic; compare response parity vs .NET for a fixed sample; canary then full cutover. Keep a fast rollback path.

This plan lets you start with a zero-DB, low-latency in-memory service and seamlessly add Mongo when dataset size, query diversity, or persistence requirements justify it—without changing the external API.

Why we recommend Nest.js over bare Express for new microservices

- Opinionated, modular architecture
  - Nest provides Modules, Controllers, and Providers (Services) with a built-in IoC/DI container. This enforces clean boundaries and makes features easy to compose and test. Express is unopinionated, so structure and lifecycle are ad hoc.
- TypeScript-first and strong typing
  - End-to-end types for DTOs, request params, and responses reduce runtime bugs and improve developer velocity. Express is JavaScript-first; TS is optional and less integrated.
- Cross-cutting concerns built-in
  - Guards (auth), Pipes (validation/transformation), Interceptors (logging/metrics/caching), and Filters (error mapping) enable clean AOP-style code. In Express you hand-roll middleware stacks and per-route logic.
- Microservices and transports
  - Out-of-the-box abstractions for Kafka, NATS, RMQ, MQTT, Redis, gRPC, and TCP—same programming model for HTTP and messages. Express provides only HTTP; messaging layers require separate libraries/patterns.
- Stability, performance options, and DX
  - Swap HTTP engine to Fastify for speed with one line. CLI scaffolding, schematics, testing utilities, and monorepo support reduce boilerplate and standardize services.
- Validation, serialization, and API contracts
  - class-validator/class-transformer integration, global validation pipes, and built-in Swagger/OpenAPI generation. With Express you wire these up manually.
- Observability and operability
  - Structured logging, exception filters, health/readiness endpoints, config and caching modules, scheduling, and ecosystem integrations (e.g., Terminus for health checks, Throttler for rate limiting).
- Easier migration from .NET patterns
  - Decorators, DI, modules, and controllers feel familiar to teams used to ASP.NET Core, speeding up adoption and keeping code maintainable.

