Here’s why we recommend Nest.js instead of bare Express for new microservices, and how it makes “templating” new services faster, safer, and more consistent across teams—followed by a drop-in service template you can reuse.

Why Nest.js over Express for microservices
- Opinionated architecture and DI
  - Modules, Controllers, and Providers with a built-in dependency injection container enforce clean boundaries. Express requires assembling structure and lifecycle by hand.
- TypeScript-first with tight DX
  - First-class TS support (decorators, metadata) gives safer, self-documenting APIs and better editor tooling. In Express, TS is optional and integrations are manual.
- Cross-cutting concerns out of the box
  - Guards (auth), Pipes (validation/transformation), Interceptors (logging/metrics/caching), and Filters (error mapping) enable AOP-style reuse without per-route boilerplate.
- Microservices transports
  - Same programming model for HTTP and async messaging (Kafka, NATS, RMQ, Redis, gRPC). Express is HTTP-only; you bolt on other transports separately.
- Productivity and consistency
  - CLI + schematics (nest g resource) generate consistent scaffolding for new modules, DTOs, tests, and Swagger decorators—ideal for templating new services.
- Operability
  - Built-in modules for config, caching, scheduling; clean health/readiness, structured error handling, and swap-in Fastify adapter for performance when needed.
- Easier migration for .NET teams
  - Decorators, DI, and controllers feel familiar to ASP.NET patterns, reducing learning curve and ensuring maintainability.

How Nest enhances templating new services
- Standardized service blueprint
  - A base “service template” repo (or Nx workspace) can include global validation, logging, error filters, health endpoints, Swagger, and security hooks. New services copy this and only add domain modules.
- Code generation
  - Use schematics to create resources with consistent DTOs, controllers, and tests: nest g resource orders —ready in minutes and aligned to team conventions.
- Reusable shared modules
  - One SharedModule can export interceptors (logging/metrics), guards (API key/JWT), and pipes (normalization), instantly available to all services.

Below is a minimal, reusable Nest “service template” you can clone to bootstrap new microservices. It includes global validation, logging, API key guard, versioning, Swagger, and a sample resource. Copy this into a template repo and run nest g resource <feature> for new endpoints.

