Here’s why we recommend Nest.js over bare Express for new microservices, and how it adds capabilities you’d otherwise assemble yourself—plus concise code snippets that show these features in practice.

What Nest.js adds on top of Express
- Opinionated architecture and dependency injection
  - Modules, Controllers, Providers (Services) and a built-in DI container give you clean boundaries, lifecycle hooks, and testability. Express leaves this up to you.
- TypeScript-first
  - Decorators and metadata give end-to-end typing for routes, DTOs, and responses, reducing runtime bugs.
- Cross-cutting concerns out of the box
  - Pipes (validation/transformation), Guards (authN/Z), Interceptors (logging/metrics/caching), Filters (error mapping) let you implement cross-cutting once and apply globally.
- Transport-agnostic microservices
  - Same programming model for HTTP and messaging (Kafka, NATS, RMQ, Redis, gRPC). Express is HTTP-only unless you add/bind separate libs.
- Developer productivity and consistency
  - CLI/schematics generate standard modules, DTOs, tests, and Swagger docs—ideal for templating new services.
- Operability
  - Config and caching modules, health/readiness patterns, structured exception handling, swap-in Fastify adapter for performance with minimal changes.

