Context and why this matters for templating new services
- The template above establishes global validation, structured logging, health/readiness, versioning, and Swagger. New services inherit these by default.
- Teams can run nest g resource <feature> to scaffold consistent modules with DTOs and tests, reducing startup time from days to hours.
- Cross-cutting concerns (API keys, metrics, caching) are implemented once and reused via Guards/Interceptors/Modules, keeping controllers lean and uniform.
- When performance needs change, you can switch to Fastify with one line and keep the rest of the app as-is.
- If/when you adopt messaging, Nest’s microservices abstractions let you add it as configuration rather than rewrite handlers.

Express baseline (contrast)
- In Express, you would hand-roll validation, auth middleware, logging, docs, and structure for each service, leading to drift and duplicated boilerplate.
- Nest builds on Express by default, so you keep the familiar HTTP engine but gain production-grade structure and capabilities out of the box.

Below is a pragmatic, end-to-end plan to port a .NET/SQL lookup service to a Nest.js (Express) microservice with an optional MongoDB backend. It covers challenges, decisions, phased execution, and operational considerations. Code snippets are included so you can bootstrap quickly and keep the API stable while switching between in-memory and Mongo modes.

Key challenges and considerations
- Data model mismatch (relational → document)
  - SQL joins/constraints don’t map 1:1. Decide embed vs reference; keep Mongoose schema strict: false initially, then tighten.
- Lookup parity and normalization
  - Preserve semantics (casing, spacing, Unicode, leading zeros like “00123”). Normalize during indexing and query.
- Performance and memory
  - XML/in-memory can be fastest but may exceed heap as data grows. Add streaming parse or move to Mongo when needed.
- Indexing strategy
  - Equality lookups: hash maps (in-memory) or single-field Mongo indexes. If range/prefix emerges, adjust accordingly.
- Freshness and reload safety
  - Debounced reloads, parse into a temp structure, atomic swap of indexes. Gate with admin token.
- Error and response parity
  - Mirror legacy response shapes (fields, status codes, null vs empty array).
- Observability and ops
  - Health/readiness, structured logs, metrics (RPS/latency/heap/reload), and rate limiting if external-facing.
- Security and secrets
  - Admin reload token, optional API key/JWT, secrets from environment or vault.
- Rollout and rollback
  - Shadow compare with legacy, canary rollout, fast rollback plan.

High-level migration plan
- Phase 0 — Discovery and contract
  - Inventory endpoints, clients, SLAs; define canonical record shape and lookup fields; lock normalization rules; define parity tests and SLOs.
- Phase 1 — In-memory Nest.js service (fastest path, no DB)
  - Implement XML ingest, build hash indexes for equality lookups, expose GET /lookup, /fields, /count, /health, POST /reload, atomic store swap.
- Phase 2 — MongoDB mode and ETL
  - Add USE_MONGO toggle. Create Mongo indexes on primary and lookup fields. Provide XML→Mongo and SQL→Mongo ETL scripts. Keep HTTP contract identical.
- Phase 3 — Hardening
  - Input validation, allowlist fields, rate limiting, structured logs, request IDs, metrics, readiness. Decide on GC/heap sizing.
- Phase 4 — Containerization and CI/CD
  - Multi-stage Dockerfile, docker-compose for local, Kubernetes manifests for prod, CI pipeline (build/test/scan/push/deploy).
- Phase 5 — Rollout
  - Shadow against .NET, canary 5–10%, monitor latency/errors/heap, full cutover with rollback switch.
- Phase 6 — Decommission
  - Remove shadow path, update runbooks/dashboards, decommission legacy.

