Nest.js is recommended for this project because it adds a production-grade application framework on top of Express that you’d otherwise have to assemble yourself. For a lookup microservice being ported from .NET/SQL, Nest gives you:

- Opinionated architecture and DI
  - Modules, Controllers, and Providers with a built-in dependency injection container. This mirrors ASP.NET Core patterns, making the transition easier and the codebase modular and testable.
- TypeScript-first and safer APIs
  - DTOs, decorators, and Validation Pipes make inputs/outputs explicit and validated by default, reducing runtime bugs and preserving parity with the legacy contract.
- Cross-cutting concerns out of the box
  - Guards (auth), Pipes (validation), Interceptors (logging/metrics), and Filters (error mapping) let you implement concerns once and apply globally without route-by-route boilerplate.
- Transport-agnostic microservices
  - Same programming model works for HTTP and async transports (Kafka/NATS/RMQ/gRPC) if you later add event-driven flows.
- Operability and team velocity
  - Health/readiness, Config/Caching/Scheduling modules, structured error handling, and Swagger/OpenAPI are integrated. The CLI scaffolds consistent services so teams can template new microservices quickly.
- Performance flexibility
  - Start on Express (default); switch to Fastify with one line if you need lower latency later.

