Below is a concise overview, followed by a complete, drop-in C# script you can place in your Unity project. It provides:
- A minimal TerrainChunkStreamer that keeps only the chunks inside a configurable render radius around the player, spawning only missing chunks and unloading far ones.
- An adapter (VistaGraphAdapter) that generates chunk data asynchronously, with a Perlin fallback. It exposes inspector fields so you can assign a Vista 2023 graph asset and fill in its runtime API calls where marked.

Overview: how TerrainChunkStreamer streams chunks
- Chunk addressing: The world is divided into a grid of chunks of size chunkSize. The player’s current “center” chunk is floor(player.position.x / chunkSize), floor(player.position.z / chunkSize).
- Desired set: Each stream tick, we compute all chunk indices within the Chebyshev distance renderRadiusChunks from the center, forming the desired set.
- Spawning missing chunks: For desired chunks not present and not already generating, we asynchronously request their data from IChunkDataProvider (VistaGraphAdapter.GetChunkAsync). When the task completes, we create a TerrainData, set heights (TerrainData.SetHeights) and, if provided, alphamaps (TerrainData.SetAlphamaps), create a terrain (Terrain.CreateTerrainGameObject), position it, and wire neighbors (Terrain.SetNeighbors).
- Unloading far chunks: Any active chunk not in the desired set is destroyed (Object.Destroy) and its inflight generation is canceled via CancellationTokenSource.
- Movement reaction: When the player moves, the “center” chunk changes. On the next tick (updateInterval seconds), the streamer updates the desired set accordingly, which spawns new chunks that came into range and unloads those that left.

