Short answer
- If your Rust code has an unmatched delimiter (for example, an extra “)”), the compiler reports it before a function-like proc macro is even invoked. There is nothing a proc macro can do to “avoid” that syntax error; you must fix the caller code.
- What you can and should avoid is letting your proc macro panic. Instead of panicking on invalid input, parse defensively and return a TokenStream that contains compile_error! with a helpful, span-attached message.

What to do in your proc macro
- Don’t use unwrap or expect inside proc macros. Use Result and convert syn::Error to tokens via to_compile_error().
- For function-like and attribute/derive macros, parse with syn and on error return err.to_compile_error().into().
- Optionally, use the proc-macro-error crate to accumulate multiple diagnostics and avoid panics.

