Below is a minimal, runnable attribute macro crate (syn v2 + quote) that parses an optional name = "..." argument and never panics. All parse errors are turned into compile_error! via syn::Error::to_compile_error(). A tiny consumer crate is included to show how malformed input produces helpful compiler errors.

Plain-English walkthrough of the parsing flow
- Item parsing (the function the attribute is attached to):
  - The macro receives two TokenStreams: attr (the attribute arguments) and item (the item being annotated).
  - We parse the item with syn::parse2::<ItemFn>(item.into()). If it’s not a function or the syntax is wrong, syn returns an error. We convert that to compile_error! by calling e.to_compile_error().into() and return immediately.
- Attribute argument parsing (nested meta):
  - We construct a parser with syn::meta::parser that visits each attribute argument (ParseNestedMeta).
  - The parser accepts either no args (#[my_attr]) or a single argument name = "string".
  - For name:
    - meta.value()?.parse()? parses the right-hand side as a syn::LitStr. If it isn’t a string literal (e.g., 123), syn reports “expected string literal.”
    - Duplicate name arguments are rejected with meta.error("duplicate argument `name`").
  - Unexpected arguments produce meta.error("unexpected argument; expected `name = \"...\"`").
  - We drive the parser using parser.parse2(attr.into()); any syn::Error is converted to compile_error! the same way.
- Code generation:
  - This minimal example just returns the original function unchanged: quote!(#func).into(). Real macros would use the parsed arguments to generate code.

Best practices in proc macros
- Span-aware diagnostics: Prefer meta.error(...) or syn::Error::new(...) so errors point at the right source spans.
- Avoid unwrap/expect: Always use Result and convert syn::Error to tokens with to_compile_error().
- Validate inputs early and clearly: Check duplicates, enforce types, and provide helpful error messages.
- Test compile-time errors: Use trybuild to write compile-fail tests that assert exact diagnostics.

