Below is a concrete, engine-agnostic recipe you can apply in Unity (URP/HDRP) and Unreal (UE5) to blend terrain and mountain colors smoothly only where their depths are close (a “contact band”), so terrain never uses the mountain’s diffuse and mountain never uses the terrain’s diffuse. You’ll render each object set into its own color+depth targets, then do a single full-screen compositing pass that mixes final colors only near the seam.

Render targets/buffers to set up

Unity (URP/HDRP):
- TerrainColor: R11G11B10 or R16G16B16A16
- TerrainDepth: R32_FLOAT (linear view-space depth, meters; or linearized in shader)
- MountainColor: R11G11B10 or R16G16B16A16
- MountainDepth: R32_FLOAT (linear)
- FinalColor: camera color (backbuffer) or an intermediate RT if needed

UE5:
- TerrainColorRT: UTextureRenderTarget2D, PF_FloatRGBA (HDR)
- TerrainDepthRT: UTextureRenderTarget2D, PF_R32_FLOAT (store linear depth)
- MountainColorRT: UTextureRenderTarget2D, PF_FloatRGBA
- MountainDepthRT: UTextureRenderTarget2D, PF_R32_FLOAT
- CompositeRT (optional if not writing straight to viewport): UTextureRenderTarget2D, PF_FloatRGBA

ASCII contact-band diagram

- The band only blends where depths are close; elsewhere the front-most object wins with no mix.

               Camera
                 \
Terrain surface -----====================----- Mountain surface
                  ^   contact band (blend)   ^
                  |                          |
             front-most wins           front-most wins

- Color bleed is prevented because shading is done independently per object into separate targets. The compositing pass never samples the other object’s diffuse or materials; it only mixes final shaded colors near the seam based on depth proximity. Outside the band, there is no mixing.

URP steps (Unity)
1) Tag terrain and mountain into different layers (e.g., TerrainLayer, MountainLayer).
2) Create two ScriptableRenderPasses that:
   - Set render target to TerrainColor/TerrainDepth and draw only TerrainLayer.
   - Set render target to MountainColor/MountainDepth and draw only MountainLayer.
3) Add a final full-screen pass (after opaques) that samples both color/depth textures and outputs to the camera color target using contact-band blending.

HDRP steps (Unity)
1) Create a Custom Pass Volume (Global).
2) Add two DrawRenderers CustomPasses:
   - One draws only TerrainLayer to TerrainColor/TerrainDepth.
   - One draws only MountainLayer to MountainColor/MountainDepth.
3) Add a FullScreen CustomPass that composites the two into the camera color buffer with contact-band blending.

UE5 steps
Option A: Two SceneCapture2D actors
1) Place two SceneCapture2D actors aligned with the main camera (copy FOV, location, rotation).
2) For the “Terrain” capture:
   - ShowOnlyActors: terrain actors only (or use HiddenActors for mountain).
   - TextureTarget: TerrainColorRT; set ClearColor = black.
   - If available, set Capture Source to “SceneColor (HDR) in RGB, Linear SceneDepth in A”, then split depth from alpha into TerrainDepthRT using a simple material pass. Otherwise, use a post-process material on the capture to write linear depth to a dedicated TerrainDepthRT.
3) Same for “Mountain” capture, writing to MountainColorRT and MountainDepthRT.
4) Create a post-process material that composites both color+depth pairs into the viewport using the contact band. Add this as a Post Process Blendable in a global PostProcessVolume.

Option B: Composure (plugin)
- Use two Composure “CG Layers” to render terrain-only and mountain-only, then add a Compositing Pass with the material below.

